<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khuc Thua Du</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/907">907</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Khúc Thừa Dụ</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khuc Thua Du was a landowner from Hong Chau in the Red River delta who seized control of the Tinh Hai circuit in 905 as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> collapsed. The weakened Tang court recognised him as military governor. He founded the autonomous rule of the Khuc family, continued by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuc-hao">Khuc Hao</a>, which is often seen as the start of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>'s path to independence.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Khúc Thừa Dụ was the head of the Khúc family, a powerful clan with a long history and tradition in Hồng Châu, Cúc Bồ, now Ninh Giang in Hải Dương. He was said to be known in the region for his wealth, hospitality and generosity, which made him an admired figure. The historian Keith Weller Taylor, in The Birth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, notes that this account of him first appears in an unofficial source (dã sử) of the 18th century, so that no firm conclusions can be drawn from it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>By then Tang authority in Tĩnh Hải quân had collapsed. The Chinese jiedushi Zeng Gun abandoned the region with his soldiers in 880, and from 880 to 905 the Tang appointed a series of short-term governors, none of whom actually held power there. Khúc Thừa Dụ then claimed the post of jiedushi. The region still belonged nominally to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a>, but it was now autonomous, with a native ruler who could decide matters without interference from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><p>Lacking the strength for a campaign against the region, the Tang emperor accepted its autonomy in the first month of 906 by giving Khúc Thừa Dụ the title of chancellor (đồng bình chương sự) together with the post of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiedushi">jiedushi</a>. The appointment, recorded in Sima Guang's Zizhi Tongjian, is the first mention of him in Chinese historical records. Taylor argues that, since power later passed smoothly to his son and there is no sign of political conflict between 880 and 906, the family was probably in control throughout those years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Khúc Thừa Dụ died in the seventh month of 907 and was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuc-hao">Khúc Hạo</a>, who continued to consolidate the autonomy of Tĩnh Hải quân. For his achievement people called him Khúc Tiên chủ, Khúc the First Master, and he is still worshipped in a temple dedicated to him in his native province. He is considered the ruler who marked the beginning of an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, which until then had been ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Trần Trọng Kim, in his Việt Nam sử lược, held that he started a revolt to claim the title.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Khúc Thừa Dụ” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 905 <b>Khuc Thua Du</b>, a powerful local landowner, seized the governorship, and his son Khuc Hao reformed taxes and administration.</blockquote><a href="/state/tinh-hai-circuit">Tinh Hai Circuit</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ninh Giang, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=20.75,106.33&amp;year=897" aria-label="Ninh Giang on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7412 1396 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Khuc Thua Du"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7862 1666h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7862 1666h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=20.75,106.33&amp;year=897">What was here in 897 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
